# Question: what does a risk cost a year (probability per year × impact),
# and does a mitigation pay off: how likely is the loss it avoids to exceed
# what it costs?
#
# Run it (it uses the shared formulas):
#   ubc --scenario risk/annual-loss
# Change an assumption, for example a cheaper mitigation:
#   ubc --set 'mitigation_cost = 5k to 15k' --scenario risk/annual-loss
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens net_benefit --scenario risk/annual-loss
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. Amounts are in euros. The
# annual loss expectancy is the loss to expect on average each year, not
# what one year costs: most years the incident does not happen, and the
# year it does it costs the whole impact. Keep the probability well below
# 1; for something that happens several times a year, use the expected
# count instead, as reliability/incident-cost does.
#
# Sources: Douglas Hubbard, How to Measure Anything (2007), for 90%
# confidence intervals and annual loss expectancy; the ranges are examples.

# Assumptions
probability = 5% to 25%           # chance of the incident in a year; your history or calibrated judgement (Hubbard)
impact = 100k to 1M               # what one incident costs: recovery, lost business, legal; your history
residual = 20% to 50%             # share of the probability left once mitigated; calibrated expert judgement
mitigation_cost = 20k to 50k      # what the mitigation costs a year: licences, time, upkeep; vendor quotes

# Calculation
loss_before = annual_loss(probability, impact)              # expected loss a year without the mitigation
loss_after = annual_loss(probability * residual, impact)    # expected loss a year with it
avoided_loss = loss_before - loss_after                     # what the mitigation saves a year
net_benefit = avoided_loss - mitigation_cost                # positive when it pays off

# Result
loss_before
loss_after
net_benefit
P(net_benefit > 0)                # how likely is the mitigation to pay off?

# Output (seed 1, 5000 samples):
#   loss_before = ≈ 34.9k   90% [8710 … 139k]   98% [4800 … 250k]   ██▆▄▃▂▂▂▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁
#   loss_after = ≈ 11.1k   90% [2550 … 48.2k]   98% [1300 … 89k]   █▇▅▃▃▂▂▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁
#   net_benefit = ≈ -8250   90% [-33.7k … 61.3k]   98% [-47k … 150k]   ▂▅█▇▄▃▂▂▂▁▁▁▁▁▁▁
#   P(net_benefit > 0) = 0.36
